229
00:25:14.390 --> 00:25:27.299
Mark Blackman: So, I… I teed this up about 3 weeks ago, 2, 3, meetings ago, but at a high level, we have a new encryption standard called TSS,

230
00:25:27.590 --> 00:25:32.620
Mark Blackman: That is going to be used, within HIRO.

231
00:25:32.800 --> 00:25:48.889
Mark Blackman: The HIP is in draft. It will be presented to the TSC at a future date, though I don't want to steal the thunder of that HIP. However, I'll provide an overview just so you have a context with regards to this request.

232
00:25:49.540 --> 00:26:00.529
Mark Blackman: So, TSS is… Gonna replace our current scheme for nodes attesting to record files, soon to be blocks.

233
00:26:00.890 --> 00:26:18.979
Mark Blackman: And what that means is the mirror node needs a way to verify that the majority of the nodes on the network, their consensus weight, more specifically, that a majority of the consensus weight has signed off on that block or record.

234
00:26:19.080 --> 00:26:37.850
Mark Blackman: And through this encryption signing process and verification process, mirror nodes can know, okay, this output data of the network is indeed attested to the protocol, basically.

235
00:26:38.250 --> 00:26:45.480
Mark Blackman: And this is obviously critical to trust and, you know, our whole, infrastructure.

236
00:26:46.140 --> 00:27:00.049
Mark Blackman: we are looking to update this to what we call TSS, and there's a few different reasons why we want to do this. Firstly, the current scheme

237
00:27:00.500 --> 00:27:07.100
Mark Blackman: In the current scheme, each node provides a signature on the record file they produce.

238
00:27:07.400 --> 00:27:17.290
Mark Blackman: And then the mirror node goes one by one and checks each signature against the hash that it was signed. So, this…

239
00:27:17.730 --> 00:27:22.570
Mark Blackman: This requires anybody who wants to verify the output of the network

240
00:27:22.710 --> 00:27:34.950
Mark Blackman: To gather multiple files, compare those until they have gone through enough signatures to meet that majority consensus weight threshold.

241
00:27:35.680 --> 00:27:39.260
Mark Blackman: So, obviously, you know, it's…

242
00:27:39.440 --> 00:27:43.439
Mark Blackman: It's… it… it takes extra time, it's extra cost.

243
00:27:43.590 --> 00:27:55.289
Mark Blackman: It does the job, but it isn't elegant, I guess, and cost-effective. And it also contributes to a lot of our end-to-end delays.

244
00:27:55.320 --> 00:28:07.129
Mark Blackman: So, you know, when a customer submits a transaction and then waits on the mirror node to actually see the result of that transaction, it increases that time.

245
00:28:07.600 --> 00:28:14.380
Mark Blackman: So what TSS does is it uses what's called an aggregated signature.

246
00:28:14.520 --> 00:28:20.809
Mark Blackman: So that all the nodes partially sign and say, yes, this is my block.

247
00:28:20.980 --> 00:28:29.089
Mark Blackman: And the network itself verifies the blocks across the nodes match

248
00:28:29.290 --> 00:28:33.239
Mark Blackman: And then, one signature is created.

249
00:28:33.440 --> 00:28:40.390
Mark Blackman: And this signature and the proof, and the verification can all reside within the block itself.

250
00:28:40.650 --> 00:28:54.239
Mark Blackman: So, what that means is, A, verifiers, mirror nodes, downstream data providers, they only have to grab the block to verify the block. So this multiple…

251
00:28:54.830 --> 00:29:02.530
Mark Blackman: Processing of multiple signatures and multiple files is, reduced, and, and

252
00:29:02.640 --> 00:29:06.329
Mark Blackman: Replaced with this one single network signature.

253
00:29:06.820 --> 00:29:10.610
Mark Blackman: And by verifying that network signature.

254
00:29:10.720 --> 00:29:17.019
Mark Blackman: You can attest that a majority of the consensus weight has signed off on that block.

255
00:29:17.180 --> 00:29:34.099
Mark Blackman: So, it not only reduces the cost and complexity, but it, more importantly, it brings a new primitive to the community, where a block itself is independently and forever verifiable by itself.

256
00:29:34.220 --> 00:29:35.970
Mark Blackman: So this is,

257
00:29:36.120 --> 00:29:45.069
Mark Blackman: You know, something, that is very useful, you know, when you get into inter-ledger communication.

258
00:29:45.700 --> 00:29:56.459
Mark Blackman: or, you know, proofs and other things. So… so this, is foundational to a lot of roadmap features, this new encryption scheme.

259
00:29:58.200 --> 00:29:59.750
Mark Blackman: Excuse me.

