00:31:30.930 --> 00:31:35.439
Mark Blackman: All right. I think that's either really good or really bad here.

273
00:31:35.580 --> 00:31:42.380
Mark Blackman: So the… There's a few different components to this signature.

274
00:31:42.810 --> 00:31:56.109
Mark Blackman: One is what I call the aggregated signature, so this allows nodes to partially sign, and then those partial signatures make the network signature. So that is called TSS.

275
00:31:56.360 --> 00:32:02.000
Mark Blackman: And, so this library has the TSS, part to it.

276
00:32:02.510 --> 00:32:06.890
Mark Blackman: Another thing a verifier needs to verify is that.

277
00:32:06.890 --> 00:32:07.380
Hendrik Ebbers (Hedera Hashgraph LLC): Excellent.

278
00:32:07.380 --> 00:32:12.760
Mark Blackman: Hey, the nodes that have signed this signature, are they actually part of the network?

279
00:32:12.760 --> 00:32:16.559
Hendrik Ebbers (Hedera Hashgraph LLC): I'm sorry, Hendrik, do you mind muting, please?

280
00:32:18.280 --> 00:32:19.140
Mark Blackman: Thank you.

281
00:32:20.460 --> 00:32:39.580
Mark Blackman: the, you have to verify, that the nodes are actually indeed part of the network, and then also that the consensus weight assigned to those nodes is correct. Because again, it's not a simple majority of nodes, it's the consensus weight

282
00:32:39.730 --> 00:32:46.619
Mark Blackman: That each node has associated based on how much staking delegations they have.

283
00:32:46.980 --> 00:32:53.389
Mark Blackman: So, another part of this, standard is actually what we call an address book proof.

284
00:32:53.820 --> 00:32:56.289
Mark Blackman: So, or a RAPS proof.

285
00:32:56.640 --> 00:33:15.059
Mark Blackman: And you want to, again, use that to verify the nodes that have made up that signature are actually nodes on the network, and then you could ensure the consensus weight, that those nodes signed with are indeed what the address book has assigned to those consensus nodes.

286
00:33:15.180 --> 00:33:27.310
Mark Blackman: So, there's… the TSS signature is a BLS signature with some extra secret sauce or internally developed tech to allow us to

287
00:33:27.460 --> 00:33:33.789
Mark Blackman: Have granular weights on those partial signatures to represent that consensus weight.

288
00:33:34.620 --> 00:33:44.739
Mark Blackman: And for that RAPS, or address book proof, we, use a SNARC, zero knowledge proof, that is recursive. So every address.

289
00:33:44.900 --> 00:33:49.180
Mark Blackman: Book release, this recursive proof will be regenerated.

290
00:33:50.350 --> 00:33:55.120
Mark Blackman: So the repo is really divided into those two, so you have the hints.

291
00:33:55.420 --> 00:34:04.850
Mark Blackman: binaries, then you have that wraps, or address book proof, and then you have some helper libraries and some utilities and such.

292
00:34:06.430 --> 00:34:24.109
Mark Blackman: I mentioned the states, we're… I would, I guess we're just dev-complete now. We are doing testing and optimization. The repo will need to go through some housekeeping and some other work, to prepare it for this migration.

293
00:34:24.170 --> 00:34:35.559
Mark Blackman: But we wanted to get approval before we got too far into that, or we actually just want to get approval so we understand, the strategy and destination of this library.

294
00:34:36.520 --> 00:34:49.479
Mark Blackman: So, who will use this? One, release engineers, building pipelines and software builds for the consensus node. Again, this will be a dependent library once TSS is introduced.

295
00:34:49.639 --> 00:34:53.729
Mark Blackman: Block verifiers and mirror nodes,

296
00:34:53.920 --> 00:35:00.650
Mark Blackman: We'll also need to use this For the verification, software.

297
00:35:00.870 --> 00:35:09.529
